  "verdict": "GO \u2014 the BG-gated slow-NMDA bistable HOLD slot with a content-agnostic Hebbian bind LATCHES the subject and CARRIES it invariantly across NOVEL filler paths: at n_fill=6 L=4 (dist 5) MARKER-gated held-out branch(verb) 1.000 >> HTM emergence-engine 0.004 (the memorise-not-generalise baseline), >> best n-gram floor 0.273, >> chance 0.250. The HOLD is load-bearing (lesion-the-hold 0.074, hold-alive 0.0949 with external input ASSERTED zero across the span), the GATE protects the latch (always-open 0.0), the BIND is load-bearing (slot-scramble 0.100), no topic->answer leakage (referent-shuffle 0.000). This is the direct surpass of the HTM held-out 0.000. NOTE: the MARKER gate's TIMING is a host scaffold (opens on the subject token) -> the memory composition is de-shortcutted + real + spiking, the gate-LEARNING is the named residual (see the learned-gate verdict). Reuse-by-import; NO sim/ edit.",
  "learned_gate_verdict": "LEARNED-GATE POSITIVE (report with caveat) \u2014 a reward-driven write-gate (REINFORCE on the verb-prediction DA, NO token-type label) LEARNED to fire LOAD on the subject and HOLD on fillers FROM STREAM STATISTICS: held-out subject precision 1.00 / recall 1.00, driving the spiking WM to held-out 1.000 (chance 0.250). CAVEAT: this stream marks the subject as a distinct BARCODE class (linearly separable from fillers), so the gate can exploit token-class rather than syntactic role; the harder residual is role/position-based gating where the same token can be subject-or-not. The update math is host; the on-substrate spiking-gate (three-factor DA-gated plasticity) is the next rung.",
